1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,500
This is DrawingOps: drawing and deliverable
control for architecture and MEP design firms.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:11,800
It works alongside AutoCAD and Revit,
and gives everyone one live record

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:15,967
of every sheet, review,
transmittal, deadline and fee stage.

4
00:00:16,867 --> 00:00:18,667
Everything revolves around the sheet.

5
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:22,774
Every sheet moves through the same
states, from planned to issued,

6
00:00:22,774 --> 00:00:26,870
and fee stages move with their sheets,
all the way to ready to invoice.

7
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:31,595
It was recorded in the interactive
prototype with fictional data.

8
00:00:31,895 --> 00:00:35,999
Outside connections, such as
Microsoft sign-in, email delivery,

9
00:00:35,999 --> 00:00:41,080
the CAD plugins and accounting,
are simulated, and labelled where they appear.

10
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:45,345
Every part is covered briefly,
and the core of daily work in detail.

11
00:00:46,495 --> 00:00:49,056
Part one, in brief: signing in, and roles.

12
00:00:49,956 --> 00:00:56,234
Staff sign in with the firm's Microsoft or Google
account, and administrators add an authenticator code.

13
00:00:56,534 --> 00:00:58,005
There is no public sign-up.

14
00:00:58,905 --> 00:01:01,782
Eight roles share one system,
from owner to accounts.

15
00:01:02,082 --> 00:01:05,744
Clients, consultants and
contractors need no account at all.

16
00:01:06,644 --> 00:01:08,277
Navigation follows the role.

17
00:01:08,577 --> 00:01:10,830
Rahul, a designer, sees his own work.

18
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:14,981
Nikhil, the owner, also sees the
portfolio, reports and billing.

19
00:01:16,131 --> 00:01:18,724
Part two, in detail: the owner's portfolio.

20
00:01:19,624 --> 00:01:25,007
The portfolio is the owner's home page:
every active project in the firm, on one page.

21
00:01:25,907 --> 00:01:27,427
Five numbers lead the page.

22
00:01:27,727 --> 00:01:30,405
20 sheets are late, and 22 are at risk.

23
00:01:30,705 --> 00:01:32,974
The oldest review has waited 5 days.

24
00:01:33,274 --> 00:01:37,511
2 people over-allocated this week,
and 2 stages are ready to invoice.

25
00:01:38,411 --> 00:01:40,947
Every number opens the exact list behind it.

26
00:01:41,247 --> 00:01:45,937
Late and at risk are computed each morning
from planned dates and the working calendar.

27
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:54,003
The projects table shows each project's fee stage,
progress, late and at-risk counts, lead and next stage date.

28
00:01:54,303 --> 00:01:57,779
Every count opens that project's
register, already filtered.

29
00:01:58,679 --> 00:02:02,559
Below, discipline workload shows
which teams are over capacity,

30
00:02:02,559 --> 00:02:07,486
and an advisory forecast lists the sheets
most likely to slip, with its reasons.

31
00:02:08,386 --> 00:02:14,365
The late list exports to Excel, PDF or CSV,
and the bell collects the owner's alerts.

32
00:02:15,515 --> 00:02:18,919
Part three, in detail:
projects, fee stages and teams.

33
00:02:19,819 --> 00:02:21,801
Opening a project shows its dashboard.

34
00:02:22,101 --> 00:02:26,276
HSR Block B, a hospital, is in
stage four, working drawings.

35
00:02:26,576 --> 00:02:30,249
The headline numbers show progress
against plan, late sheets,

36
00:02:30,249 --> 00:02:35,006
sheets waiting for check, unacknowledged
transmittals and open client comments.

37
00:02:35,906 --> 00:02:38,915
The burn-up chart compares
planned and issued sheets,

38
00:02:38,915 --> 00:02:43,853
and the stage timeline shows each fee stage
with its share of the fee, dates and state.

39
00:02:44,753 --> 00:02:48,640
Priya, the project lead, creates
projects from a fee-stage template.

40
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:56,741
The stage plan mirrors the appointment letter: each stage's
share and amount, dates, required issue purposes, and senior QC.

41
00:02:57,041 --> 00:02:59,733
Stages move by themselves as their sheets move.

42
00:03:00,633 --> 00:03:03,580
The team tab controls who can access the project,

43
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:08,212
with each person's role and load,
and the activity feed records every change.

44
00:03:09,362 --> 00:03:11,878
Part four, in detail: the drawing register.

45
00:03:12,778 --> 00:03:16,837
The register is the heart of DrawingOps:
every sheet the firm owes,

46
00:03:16,837 --> 00:03:20,775
with its owner, checker, state,
planned date, revision and flags.

47
00:03:21,075 --> 00:03:24,191
All one hundred and thirty-two
sheets render instantly.

48
00:03:25,091 --> 00:03:30,949
Search covers numbers, titles and owners:
OT duct finds the operating-theatre ductwork.

49
00:03:31,249 --> 00:03:34,304
Filters combine, and here are the late HVAC sheets.

50
00:03:35,204 --> 00:03:38,798
Owners, checkers and dates edit
in place, audited and undoable.

51
00:03:39,098 --> 00:03:46,212
Selecting sheets brings up the bulk bar: assign, shift dates
on the working calendar, change state, issue or export.

52
00:03:47,112 --> 00:03:48,554
Most firms start from Excel.

53
00:03:48,854 --> 00:03:55,369
The import wizard checks every row before anything
is created, and explains each problem in plain words.

54
00:03:56,269 --> 00:04:03,514
PDFs are matched to their sheets by file name, or by
reading a scan's title block, and become draft revisions.

55
00:04:03,814 --> 00:04:08,014
A file that breaks the naming rule
cannot be issued until it is renamed.

56
00:04:08,914 --> 00:04:15,529
Sheet lists can also sync from Revit and AutoCAD,
simulated here, as proposals that a lead accepts.

57
00:04:16,429 --> 00:04:17,974
Each sheet has its own page.

58
00:04:18,274 --> 00:04:21,923
M-205 is in progress and late by 2 working days.

59
00:04:22,223 --> 00:04:28,612
The current drawing opens in the built-in viewer, beside
every review and a record of who holds which revision.

60
00:04:29,512 --> 00:04:34,889
An older revision shows a superseded banner,
and every download of it is stamped SUPERSEDED.

61
00:04:36,039 --> 00:04:38,541
Part five, in brief: drafting and checking.

62
00:04:39,441 --> 00:04:43,567
Rahul's My work lists his sheets by
due date, with the next action on each.

63
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:45,659
Starting one is a single click.

64
00:04:46,559 --> 00:04:50,918
Submitting M-206 for check needs the
discipline's pre-check list ticked.

65
00:04:51,218 --> 00:04:54,314
Karthik, the HVAC checker, is notified at once.

66
00:04:55,214 --> 00:05:00,071
Anil checks his queue oldest first,
and every return carries a comment or redline.

67
00:05:00,371 --> 00:05:05,465
Approving A-107 sends it on to
senior QC, which stage four requires.

68
00:05:06,365 --> 00:05:11,394
Sunita, the senior reviewer, approves
it, and A-107 is approved for issue.

69
00:05:12,544 --> 00:05:15,410
Part six, in detail: issue, and issue tracking.

70
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:20,395
Deepa, the document controller,
sees the approved sheets ready to issue.

71
00:05:21,295 --> 00:05:24,317
The issue composer reserves
the next transmittal number.

72
00:05:24,617 --> 00:05:29,903
A-104 is blocked by the naming
rule, and E-102 has no PDF yet.

73
00:05:30,803 --> 00:05:35,484
One click renames A-104's file,
and all approved sheets are selected.

74
00:05:35,784 --> 00:05:41,581
Every issue needs a purpose, here good for construction,
and recipients get a secure link by email.

75
00:05:42,481 --> 00:05:46,089
The cover sheet previews exactly
as recipients will receive it.

76
00:05:46,989 --> 00:05:50,371
One confirmation issues the
set on a single transmittal.

77
00:05:50,671 --> 00:05:58,424
In the same step, earlier revisions are superseded, recipients
are emailed, and anyone holding an older revision is told.

78
00:05:59,324 --> 00:06:05,329
The transmittal log tracks every issue, its recipients
and acknowledgements, including bounced emails.

79
00:06:05,629 --> 00:06:08,611
The matrix shows which
revision each recipient holds.

80
00:06:09,511 --> 00:06:14,407
Each transmittal records delivery, download
and acknowledgement times per recipient.

81
00:06:14,707 --> 00:06:21,005
Unacknowledged transmittals are flagged, and a sent
transmittal is never edited: a correction is a new one.

82
00:06:22,155 --> 00:06:24,519
Part seven, in detail: the client's side.

83
00:06:24,819 --> 00:06:26,844
No account, and nothing to install.

84
00:06:27,744 --> 00:06:33,511
The client receives an ordinary email with a secure
link, shown here in the demonstration mailbox.

85
00:06:34,411 --> 00:06:38,132
The link opens the transmittal,
with every drawing and its revision.

86
00:06:38,432 --> 00:06:42,117
Drawings download one at a time,
or as a zip with the cover sheet.

87
00:06:43,017 --> 00:06:45,533
Acknowledging receipt records the date and time.

88
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:52,249
On site, a phone view shows only the current issued
set, so nobody builds from a superseded sheet.

89
00:06:53,149 --> 00:06:56,368
Back in DrawingOps, Deepa
already sees the acknowledgement.

90
00:06:57,518 --> 00:07:00,828
Part eight, in brief: client
comments and stage approvals.

91
00:07:01,728 --> 00:07:05,648
Client comments from any channel are
logged, linked to sheets and assigned.

92
00:07:05,948 --> 00:07:09,953
Each closes only with a written response,
and overdue ones are flagged.

93
00:07:10,853 --> 00:07:15,614
When every deliverable of a stage is issued,
Priya records the client's approval,

94
00:07:15,614 --> 00:07:20,023
with the letter as evidence, and the
Mysuru stage becomes ready to invoice.

95
00:07:21,173 --> 00:07:23,585
Part nine, in detail: billing signals.

96
00:07:24,485 --> 00:07:29,960
The administrator connects the accounting system once,
with a webhook address that is checked for safety.

97
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:31,487
It is simulated here.

98
00:07:32,387 --> 00:07:36,260
Meera, in Accounts, sees what is
ready to invoice on her home page.

99
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:40,218
Each signal shows the stage,
its share of the fee, the amount,

100
00:07:40,218 --> 00:07:45,175
the wait, and the client approval, including
the Mysuru stage approved a moment ago.

101
00:07:46,075 --> 00:07:52,913
Signals export as CSV, or push to the accounting
system as a signed message, shown here instead of sent.

102
00:07:53,813 --> 00:07:59,176
When the invoice is raised, Meera records its
reference, and the stage's fee percentage locks.

103
00:08:00,076 --> 00:08:06,635
Invoicing and tax stay in the accounting software,
and Accounts never sees drawings or individual hours.

104
00:08:07,785 --> 00:08:09,901
Part ten, in brief: everything else.

105
00:08:10,801 --> 00:08:16,865
Workload shows planned hours against capacity,
eight weeks ahead, and weekly timesheets build on it.

106
00:08:17,765 --> 00:08:24,326
On site, RFIs, submittals, site reports, change
orders and clashes link to the sheets they affect.

107
00:08:25,226 --> 00:08:30,465
Control K finds anything, and knowledge search
finds the reasoning behind past decisions.

108
00:08:31,365 --> 00:08:35,275
Reports track the four baseline
measures the firm agreed to improve.

109
00:08:36,175 --> 00:08:41,510
Ten automation rules run themselves, and running
a sweep twice never sends anything twice.

110
00:08:42,410 --> 00:08:48,304
Forecasts and AI drawing checks are advisory,
labelled, and gated on the firm's own history.

111
00:08:48,604 --> 00:08:50,368
The figures here are simulated.

112
00:08:51,268 --> 00:08:57,938
And administrators manage users, rules and integrations,
with every change in an insert-only audit trail.

113
00:08:59,088 --> 00:09:01,052
Part eleven, in detail: email.

114
00:09:01,352 --> 00:09:04,292
DrawingOps works through the
inbox people already use.

115
00:09:04,591 --> 00:09:10,027
It sends every alert and transmittal, logs
client emails that come in, and tracks each one.

116
00:09:10,927 --> 00:09:15,210
The demonstration mailbox collects
every email the product sends:

117
00:09:15,210 --> 00:09:19,756
transmittals, notifications,
revision notices, digests and summaries.

118
00:09:20,057 --> 00:09:25,241
In production they go through the firm's own
mail server; here, nothing is actually sent.

119
00:09:26,141 --> 00:09:29,392
Filtering by recipient shows
what each person received.

120
00:09:29,692 --> 00:09:33,415
When Rahul submitted M-206,
Karthik, the HVAC checker,

121
00:09:33,415 --> 00:09:38,241
was emailed at once, with a button
that opens the sheet in DrawingOps.

122
00:09:39,141 --> 00:09:41,952
Meera's ready-to-invoice alert arrived the same way.

123
00:09:42,252 --> 00:09:45,713
Must-not-miss alerts like this
one are always sent instantly.

124
00:09:46,613 --> 00:09:49,133
Transmittal emails are tracked per recipient.

125
00:09:49,433 --> 00:09:52,662
One email on the previous HSR transmittal bounced.

126
00:09:52,962 --> 00:09:55,730
It shows here, and Deepa resends it with one click.

127
00:09:56,630 --> 00:09:57,873
Email comes in, too.

128
00:09:58,173 --> 00:10:05,377
A client email forwarded to the project's own address is logged
as a comment, matched to the sender; that is simulated here.

129
00:10:05,677 --> 00:10:08,372
Unknown senders wait in a queue until accepted.

130
00:10:09,272 --> 00:10:15,048
Each person chooses instant alerts, a morning
digest, or none, with critical alerts locked on.

131
00:10:15,348 --> 00:10:18,379
Rahul's digest gathers the rest into one email.

132
00:10:19,279 --> 00:10:22,785
And every Monday, the owner
gets a weekly summary by email.

133
00:10:23,935 --> 00:10:26,126
Part twelve: how the roles work together.

134
00:10:26,426 --> 00:10:32,941
Each role owns one part of a sheet's journey, and every
hand-off tells the next person, in the app and by email.

135
00:10:33,241 --> 00:10:34,771
Nobody chases anybody.

136
00:10:35,671 --> 00:10:38,312
In this recording, that chain ran end to end.

137
00:10:38,612 --> 00:10:44,648
Sunita's bell shows the senior QC request
for A-107, sent the moment Anil approved it.

138
00:10:45,548 --> 00:10:56,126
Deepa was told A-107 was ready to issue, and that
the client had acknowledged 2026-014-TR-0033.

139
00:10:57,026 --> 00:11:02,668
Priya, the project lead, gets a copy of every
transmittal and acknowledgement on her projects.

140
00:11:03,568 --> 00:11:07,434
Meera was told the moment the
Mysuru stage was ready to invoice.

141
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:11,705
And Nikhil sees the whole chain
in the project's activity feed.

142
00:11:12,005 --> 00:11:16,499
Each person sees only what their role
allows, so hand-offs are quick and safe.

143
00:11:17,649 --> 00:11:20,904
Underneath, permissions are enforced on the server,

144
00:11:20,904 --> 00:11:26,840
issued files never change, the audit trail is
insert-only, and every project exports in full.

145
00:11:27,740 --> 00:11:32,860
DrawingOps gives the whole firm one live record
of every sheet, from planned to invoiced.

146
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:34,864
It is built by LatentIQ.

147
00:11:35,164 --> 00:11:38,657
To see it with your own projects,
talk to the LatentIQ team.

148
00:11:38,957 --> 00:11:40,172
Thank you for watching.

